  "source": "{\"cells\":[{\"cell_type\":\"markdown\",\"id\":\"3438e85d\",\"metadata\":{},\"source\":\"# Signed-digit arithmetic and polynomial QROM\\n\\n**A new three-task compilation, beyond our observed 5.67004 baseline.** The resource table below is generated by full official verification. Its combined value is a projection; the notebook's Kaggle score badge records the actual competition result.\\n\\nThree changes drive this revision:\\n\\n- **92-qubit inverse square root, 1472 static Toffoli.** We combine the calibrated coefficients of Kozykorpesh, derivative splitting of Rudra Bhaskar, and scheduling integrated by Kirill and Alex Chilton. Our carry borrowing and promised-input simplification reduce the remaining workspace and gates. The coefficient table and isolated comparisons are included.\\n- **Polynomial QROM.** Our exact GF(2) bank compression is followed by a new algebraic-normal-form schedule for lookup and measurement cleanup. Chemistry falls from **72,408 to 71,958 Toffoli**, with the factorization and all angle words unchanged.\\n- **Fast DQI implication compiler.** A direct C++ pass reproduces Kozykorpesh's 231-Toffoli improvement in about three seconds on Kaggle, preserving measurements and classical corrections. Source, branch regressions and full verification evidence are provided.\\n\\nAll three unchanged official verifiers must pass before the archive is produced. Generators and the 18 archive members are pinned by SHA-256. GPU and Internet access are unnecessary. Credits and the limits of the inherited binary dependency appear at the end.\\n\"},{\"cell_type\":\"code\",\"execution_count\":null,\"id\":\"717004a1\",\"metadata\":{\"_kg_hide-input\":true,\"collapsed\":true,\"hide_input\":true,\"jupyter\":{\"source_hidden\":true}},\"outputs\":[],\"source\":\"# notebook: hide_input\\nfrom __future__ import annotations\\n\\nimport gc\\nimport hashlib\\nimport importlib.util\\nimport json\\nimport math\\nimport os\\nfrom pathlib import Path\\nimport platform\\nimport shutil\\nimport sys\\nimport time\\nimport zipfile\\n\\nos.environ.update(OMP_NUM_THREADS='1', OPENBLAS_NUM_THREADS='1', MKL_NUM_THREADS='1')\\nimport numpy as np\\nimport pandas as pd\\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\\nfrom IPython.display import display\\n\\nINPUT = Path('/kaggle/input')\\nWORK = Path('/kaggle/working') if INPUT.exists() else Path.cwd() / 'runs' / 'notebook'\\nWORK.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)\\nCOMP = next((p for p in [INPUT / 'competitions/google-verifiable-quantum-circuit-compilation',\\n                         INPUT / 'google-verifiable-quantum-circuit-compilation',\\n                         Path.cwd() / 'data/competition'] if (p / 'verifiers').exists()), None)\\nif COMP is None:\\n    raise FileNotFoundError('Attach the competition data listed in the notebook Input panel.')\\nsys.path[:0] = [str(COMP / 'kickmix'), str(COMP / 'verifiers')]\\nimport kickmix as km\\nfrom invsqrt import verify_submission_invsqrt as IV\\nfrom dqi import verify_submission_dqi as DV\\nfrom dfthcsos.verify import verify as verify_chemistry\\n\\nRUN_FULL_VERIFICATION = True\\nSALT = 'vqcc-signed-digit-memory-v1-20261023'\\nWORKERS = min(3, os.cpu_count() or 1)\\nREPORTS = WORK / 'reports'; REPORTS.mkdir(exist_ok=True)\\nFIGURES = WORK / 'figures'; FIGURES.mkdir(exist_ok=True)\\nFINAL = WORK / 'submission'; FINAL.mkdir(exist_ok=True)\\n\\nREFERENCE_COSTS = {'invsqrt': 2076774.233715282, 'dqi': 24439473914136.668, 'dfthcsos': 3355599588.3155365}\\n\\ndef digest(path):\\n    with Path(path).open('rb') as stream:\\n        return hashlib.file_digest(stream, 'sha256').hexdigest()\\n\\ndef save_json(path, value):\\n    def encode(item):\\n        if isinstance(item, np.ndarray):\\n            return item.tolist()\\n        if isinstance(item, np.generic):\\n            return item.item()\\n        if isinstance(item, Path):\\n            return str(item)\\n        raise TypeError(type(item).__name__)\\n    Path(path).write_text(json.dumps(value, indent=2, default=encode), encoding='utf-8')\\n\\ndef measured(name, function):\\n    started = time.monotonic()\\n    result = function()\\n    result['wall_seconds'] = time.monotonic() - started\\n    save_json(REPORTS / f'{name}.json', result)\\n    return result\\n\\nenvironment = {'python': sys.version, 'numpy': np.__version__, 'kickmix': getattr(km, '__version__', 'unknown'),\\n               'platform': platform.platform(), 'workers': WORKERS,\\n               'full_verification': RUN_FULL_VERIFICATION}\\nsave_json(REPORTS / 'environment.json', environment)\\nprint(json.dumps(environment, indent=2))\\n\\nCOLORS = {'ink': '#142B43', 'blue': '#207F9E', 'orange': '#D98D38', 'green': '#29876A', 'muted': '#9BAAB7'}\\nplt.rcParams.update({'figure.dpi': 130, 'font.size': 11, 'axes.spines.top': False,\\n                     'axes.spines.right': False, 'axes.titleweight': 'bold',\\n                     'text.color': COLORS['ink'], 'axes.labelcolor': COLORS['ink'],\\n                     'figure.facecolor': '#FFFFFF', 'savefig.facecolor': '#FFFFFF'})\\n\"},{\"cell_type\":\"markdown\",\"id\":\"fdae2f8a\",\"metadata\":{},\"source\":\"## Inputs and reproducibility\\n\\nThe Input panel attaches the official competition data and our previous public output. We reuse its exact DQI binary and chemistry factor NPZ, checking both by SHA-256. This run then rewrites the decoder and rebuilds inverse square root and all 15 ANGLE circuits from the included source. Numeric factors remain unchanged. All computation runs inside Kaggle on CPU with its existing C++17 compiler.\\n\"},{\"cell_type\":\"code\",\"execution_count\":null,\"id\":\"b2e7952f\",\"metadata\":{\"_kg_hide-input\":true,\"collapsed\":true,\"hide_input\":true,\"jupyter\":{\"source_hidden\":true}},\"outputs\":[],\"source\":\"# notebook: hide_input\\n# Reuse only the exact DQI binary and chemistry factorization from the previous public release.\\n# The inverse-square-root circuit and every ANGLE circuit are rebuilt from source.\\nBASE_MEMBERS = {'dqi.kmb': {'file': 'dqi.kmb',\\n             'bytes': 422062449,\\n             'sha256': 'b1b8fb73672cf9a9f6ec0c2a8a4e3b77ab54c72b7e4ef3073bcd67dba271a1be'},\\n 'dfthcsos/submission.npz': {'file': 'dfthcsos/submission.npz',\\n                             'bytes': 5734744,\\n                             'sha256': 'c5b4165e7b1dcacf1d287746642414a9ba6086b4279e5ede2bb73e21e6aebd98'}}\\n\\nPUBLIC = WORK / 'temporary'; PUBLIC.mkdir(exist_ok=True)\\nsource_archive = None\\nfor candidate in INPUT.rglob('submission.zip'):\\n    with zipfile.ZipFile(candidate) as archive:\\n        if not set(BASE_MEMBERS).issubset(archive.namelist()):\\n            continue\\n        if any(archive.getinfo(name).file_size != row['bytes'] for name,row in BASE_MEMBERS.items()):\\n            continue\\n        matches = True\\n        for name, row in BASE_MEMBERS.items():\\n            with archive.open(name) as stream:\\n                if hashlib.file_digest(stream, 'sha256').hexdigest() != row['sha256']:\\n                    matches = False\\n                    break\\n        if matches:\\n            source_archive = candidate\\n            break\\nif source_archive is None:\\n    raise FileNotFoundError('Attach the previous public Signed-Digit Memory release; the DQI and NPZ hashes must match.')\\nwith zipfile.ZipFile(source_archive) as archive:\\n    for name,row in BASE_MEMBERS.items():\\n        relative = Path(name)\\n        assert not relative.is_absolute() and '..' not in relative.parts\\n        target = FINAL / relative; target.parent.mkdir(parents=True,exist_ok=True)\\n        with archive.open(name) as stream, target.open('wb') as output:\\n            shutil.copyfileobj(stream, output, 8 << 20)\\n        assert digest(target) == row['sha256']\\nsave_json(REPORTS / 'public_source.json', {\\n    'notebook': 'https://www.kaggle.com/code/anazemcev/quantum-circuits-signed-digit-memory?scriptVersionId=361481141',\\n    'version': 2, 'members': BASE_MEMBERS, 'invsqrt_reused': False, 'angle_circuits_reused': False,\\n    'upstream_decoder': 'k256.dev; our bounded Boolean-polynomial simplifier',\\n    'chemistry_factors': 'Kozykorpesh compression/rotations; our effective-norm optimization',\\n    'input_note': 'Previous public output is mounted inside Kaggle; reused members are checked by SHA-256. No HTTP downloads.'})\\nprint('Reused DQI and factorization members reproduced by SHA-256:', len(BASE_MEMBERS))\\n\"},{\"cell_type\":\"markdown\",\"id\":\"6b067d57\",\"metadata\":{},\"source\":\"## Arithmetic: change the representation, then borrow memory\\n\\nSumit Chongder's signed-digit construction normalizes the input to a mantissa and binary shift. An eight-bit address selects a quadratic coefficient row. Its centered offset is\\n\\n$$\\\\delta_c=\\\\sum_{i=0}^{13}(2d_i-1)\\\\,2^{i-22}.$$\\n\\nDigits of $-1$ or $+1$ allow an uncontrolled Gidney adder surrounded by CNOT flips. Per-product rounding defines the integer Horner pipeline. This run uses Kozykorpesh's published `Cfg(8, 19, 27, 1, (5,))` and explicit 256-row calibrated integer table. The open calibration algorithm is included; the default run uses fixed integers for reproducibility and still certifies every promised input.\\n\\n### Split a derivative without changing its value\\n\\nRudra Bhaskar's construction represents the 18-bit derivative using a 12-bit residual and a table-dependent coarse word:\\n\\n$$D=R+2^{11}B\\n=(R\\\\bmod2^{11})+2^{11}\\\\bigl(B+\\\\lfloor R/2^{11}\\\\rfloor\\\\bigr).$$\\n\\nThe inner Horner rows update the shorter residual. One small addition merges its overflow into the coarse word before the outer rows. Bounds are checked over all **4,194,304 normalized address/offset combinations**: $0\\\\le R<2^{12}$ and the merged coarse value fits seven bits. The split preserves the original rounded integer pipeline exactly.\\n\\nAgainst our earlier 91-qubit implementation with the same coefficients, the split stores 19 derivative bits instead of 18. The 16 inner additions shrink by six bits, saving 96 Toffoli; the two coarse-word joins add 12. The net change is **84 fewer Toffoli for one extra qubit**. The complete arithmetic cost falls from about 170,500 to 164,646. The historical comparison in the plot makes that resource trade visible.\\n\\nKirill integrated this representation with Alex Chilton's shared-prefix normalization and temporary-AND moves into empty shift targets. Their measurement-based inverses retain the required phase corrections. The static high output is phased and cleared before Horner arithmetic. For a split $Y_{\\\\rm raw}=L+2^S H$ and one discarded low bit,\\n\\n$$Y=2\\\\lfloor L/2\\\\rfloor+2^S H,\\\\qquad\\n e^{2\\\\pi i\\\\alpha Y}=e^{2\\\\pi i\\\\alpha\\\\,2\\\\lfloor L/2\\\\rfloor}\\\\,e^{2\\\\pi i\\\\alpha\\\\,2^S H}.$$\\n\\nClearing the high word early makes those wires available during the more demanding arithmetic stage.\\n\\n### Our two additional changes\\n\\n**Borrow one proven constant.** Normalization makes `x[21]` equal to one. The coefficient address and centered offset use other bits. We flip it to zero, use it as one clean adder carry, return it to zero, then restore one before inverse normalization. This reduces the fixed-table schedule from **93 to 92 qubits** with unchanged gate counts. The last carry is folded into the top sum bit as in the attributed memory schedule.\\n\\n**Use the original input promise.** Every allowed input satisfies\\n\\n$$1\\\\le x\\\\le3{,}133{,}452<3\\\\cdot2^{20}.$$\\n\\nThus $x_{21}x_{20}=0$ and\\n\\n$$(1\\\\oplus x_{21})(1\\\\oplus x_{20})=1\\\\oplus x_{21}\\\\oplus x_{20}.$$\\n\\nThe first prefix AND of these inverted bits can use X/CNOT gates. The same replacement is valid in the final 16-bit phase fixup **after inverse normalization has restored the original input**. Earlier fixups retain generic predicates. It removes **two static and 1.5 expected Toffoli**, without changing the integer model.\\n\\nThe ablation below holds the coefficient table fixed: derivative-split schedule, carry borrowing, then the input-domain simplification. Development checks cover all **3,133,452** promised inputs, the split bounds and **32,768** fresh phase/model, restored-input and clean-workspace shots. Full official circuit verification follows later in this run.\\n\"},{\"cell_type\":\"code\",\"execution_count\":null,\"id\":\"f2cd6d32\",\"metadata\":{\"_kg_hide-input\":true,\"_kg_hide-output\":true,\"collapsed\":true,\"hide_input\":true,\"jupyter\":{\"source_hidden\":true}},\"outputs\":[],\"source\":\"%%writefile signed_digit_lab.py\\n# SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0\\n# New derivative-split integration; original upstream notices retained below.\\n# Sumit Chongder: centered signed-digit generator and integer pipeline,361357149.\\n# Rudra Bhaskar: derivative split, top-carry reuse, static-high checkpoint,361445283.\\n# https://www.kaggle.com/code/rudrabhaskar/highest-public-score-direct-polynomial-phasing?scriptVersionId=361445283\\n# Alex Chilton: shared prefix and empty-target temporary-AND shifts,361472684.\\n# Kirill Tymchishin: exact Rudra/Alex integration used as the95Q source baseline,361491814.\\n# https://www.kaggle.com/code/kirilltym/quantum-circuits-qrom-tower42?scriptVersionId=361491814\\n# Kozykorpesh: explicit8,19,27,1 coefficient model,datasetVersionId21136269.\\n# Our changes: generic exact coefficient widths/left shifts, normalized-input\\n# borrowing on this derivative schedule, and the proven original-input rewrite.\\n# The derivative split and its precision calibrations are credited upstream.\\n\\n# SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0\\n# Arithmetic research candidate. Retained upstream attribution:\\n# Sumit Chongder: readable centered signed-digit generator and integer pipeline.\\n# https://www.kaggle.com/code/sumitchongder/notebook2f196e646b?scriptVersionId=361357149\\n# anazemcev / Artem1410: early high-word phase, folded carry and normalized-input borrowing.\\n# https://www.kaggle.com/code/anazemcev/quantum-circuits-signed-digit-memory?scriptVersionId=361422248\\n# Kozykorpesh: exact reachable-domain calibration, Cfg(8,19,27,1), explicit table.\\n# https://www.kaggle.com/code/kozykappa/quantum-ai-compact-arithmetic-banked-qrom?scriptVersionId=361456406\\n# Quantum Circuit Toolbox datasetVersionId 21136269, code/domain_tables.py and coefficients.json.\\n# Alex Chilton: shared prefix flags and temporary-AND moves into zero destinations.\\n# https://www.kaggle.com/code/alexchilton/where-the-toffolis-go-anatomy-a-cheaper-invsqrt?scriptVersionId=361472684\\n# This revision combines credited methods. No upstream gain is claimed as original.\\n# Added own exact rewrite: the original input bound proves x[21]&x[20]==0.\\n# Linearize the first conjunction in normalization and the final input phase fixup.\\n\\n# Attribution: Sumit Chongder, Apache-2.0 as published.\\n# https://www.kaggle.com/code/sumitchongder/notebook2f196e646b\\n# Read-only source downloaded from latest version 2. Kirill's v9 cites v1.\\n# This isolated lab regenerates the circuit from readable source; no binaries or\\n# remote notebook outputs are inputs. Our added exact integer certificate and\\n# reporting helpers and memory-schedule adaptations follow the original generator.\\n\\\"\\\"\\\"Builds an optimized kickmix circuit for the invsqrt benchmark (Benchmark I).\\n\\n|x>|0> -> exp(2 pi i / sqrt(x)) |x>|0>,   x in [1, 3133452], 22-bit register 'x'.\\n\\nDesign (all arithmetic exactly mirrored by the numpy model below):\\n  1. Normalize x by single-bit binary stages (16, 8, 4, 2, 1) into M = x_norm / 2^21 in [1, 2);\\n     flags f0..f4 hold the shift count s' in binary.  e = f0 (parity), s = s' >> 1.\\n     1/sqrt(x) = 2^(s-10) * y,  y = 2^((e-1)/2) / sqrt(M)  in (0.5, 1].\\n  2. One 256-entry QROM indexed by (7 mantissa bits, e) XORs quadratic coefficients directly into\\n     the working registers (no separate coefficient copies).\\n  3. Centered signed-digit Horner: delta_c = sum_i (2 d_i - 1) 2^(i-22) over the 14 low bits.\\n     R = K1 - C2*delta_c,  y = K0 - R*delta_c.  Each row is an UNcontrolled Gidney addition\\n     wrapped in CNOT flips (Y - A = ~(~Y + A)), with a rounding carry-in.\\n  4. The y register is split: rows only touch a 21-bit low register L; the constant high part\\n     sits in a separate register and the overflow bit is merged in before phasing.\\n  5. Binary de-normalization (shift by s), one Z_POW per bit, then exact mirror uncompute.\\n     Lookup uncompute is measurement based (del_lookup); normalization flags are cleared by HMR\\n     with classically conditioned phase fixups.\\nUsage: python signed_digit_lab.py OUT.kmb [--baseline] (requires kickmix and NumPy)\\n\\\"\\\"\\\"\\nimport sys, math, functools, types\\nfrom fractions import Fraction\\nimport numpy as np\\nimport kickmix as km\\n\\nW = 22; MB = 21; DB = 14; NSEG = 128\\nF2, F1, FYR, GY, DROP2 = 8, 19, 27, 1, (5,)     # precision configuration (verified)\\n\\n# ----------------------------------------------------------------- numpy model / tables\\n@functools.lru_cache(None)\\ndef fitc(lo, hi, mid, scale, deg=2, iters=80):\\n    f = lambda M: scale / np.sqrt(M)\\n    d = np.linspace(lo - mid, hi - mid, 3001)\\n    A = np.vander(d, deg + 1, increasing=True)\\n    w = np.sqrt(mid + d) / scale\\n    wt = np.ones_like(d)\\n    for _ in range(iters):                       # Lawson iterations -> weighted minimax\\n        sw = w * np.sqrt(wt)\\n        coef = np.linalg.lstsq(A * sw[:, None], f(mid + d) * sw, rcond=None)[0]\\n        err = np.abs((A @ coef - f(mid + d)) * w)\\n        wt = wt * err; wt /= wt.sum()\\n    return coef\\n\\nclass Cfg:\\n    def __init__(self, f2, f1, fyr, gy, drop2=()):\\n        self.f2, self.f1, self.fyr, self.gy = f2, f1, fyr, gy\\n        self.fy = fyr + gy\\n        self.w2 = f2 - 1\\n        self.sh2 = [f2 + 22 - i - f1 for i in range(DB)]\\n        self.sh1 = [f1 + 22 - i - self.fy for i in range(DB)]\\n        self.drop2 = set(drop2)\\n    def rrow_list(self):\\n        return [i for i in reversed(range(DB)) if i not in self.drop2 and self.sh2[i] < self.w2]\\n\\ndef rd(v, s):\\n    if s < 0: return v << (-s)\\n    r = v >> s\\n    if s >= 1: r = r + ((v >> (s - 1)) & 1)\\n    return r\\n\\ndef pipeline(cfg, K2, K1, d):\\n    sig = [2 * ((d >> i) & 1) - 1 for i in range(DB)]\\n    R = np.full_like(d, K1)\\n    for i in cfg.rrow_list():\\n        R = R - sig[i] * rd(K2, cfg.sh2[i])\\n    Q = np.zeros_like(d)\\n    for i in reversed(range(DB)):\\n        Q = Q + sig[i] * rd(R, cfg.sh1[i])\\n    return R, Q\\n\\nMAX_X = 3_133_452\\n\\ndef valid_residuals(e,a):\\n    d=np.arange(1<<DB,dtype=np.int64);xn=(1<<MB)+(a<<DB)+d\\n    mask=(d%2==0) if e else ((xn<=MAX_X)|(d%4==0))\\n    return d[mask]\\n\\ndef calibrate_segment(cfg,e,a,c_radius=1,b_radius=3):\\n    d=valid_residuals(e,a);lo=1+a*2.**(DB-MB);mid=lo+((1<<DB)-1)/2**(MB+1)\\n    scale=2**((e-1)/2);co=fitc(lo,lo+2.**(DB-MB),mid,scale)\\n    f=scale/np.sqrt(lo+d*2.**-MB);target=f*2**cfg.fy;w=1/f\\n    K2c=round(co[2]*2**cfg.f2);K1c=round(-co[1]*2**cfg.f1);best=None;quant=1<<cfg.gy\\n    for K2 in range(max(0,K2c-c_radius),min((1<<cfg.w2)-1,K2c+c_radius)+1):\\n        for K1 in range(K1c-b_radius,K1c+b_radius+1):\\n            R,Q=pipeline(cfg,K2,K1,d)\\n            if R.min()<0:continue\\n            vals=target+Q;center=.5*(vals.min()+vals.max())+.5*(quant-1)\\n            k0s=np.arange(math.floor(center)-quant-1,math.ceil(center)+quant+2,dtype=np.int64)\\n            # The stored high anchor and the carry must keep the leading one fixed.\\n            k0s=k0s[k0s <= (1<<cfg.fy)-1+int(Q.min())]\\n            if len(k0s)==0:continue\\n            y=((k0s[:,None]-Q)>>cfg.gy)<<cfg.gy\\n            scores=np.max(abs(y-target)*w,axis=1)/2**cfg.fy;i=int(scores.argmin())\\n            v=float(scores[i]);K0=int(k0s[i])\\n            if best is None or v<best[0]:best=(v,K2,K1,K0)\\n    if best is None:raise RuntimeError('No legal coefficient triple')\\n    return best\\n\\ndef regenerate_tables_exact(cfg,refine_threshold=.95):\\n    tabs={};ratios=[]\\n    for e in(0,1):\\n        for a in range(128):\\n            best=calibrate_segment(cfg,e,a)\\n            if best[0]*2**24>refine_threshold:\\n                alt=calibrate_segment(cfg,e,a,c_radius=4,b_radius=7)\\n                if alt[0]<best[0]:best=alt\\n            tabs[(e,a)]=best[1:];ratios.append(best[0]*2**24)\\n    return tabs,dict(worst_ratio=max(ratios),worst_key=[int(np.argmax(ratios))//128,int(np.argmax(ratios))%128],reachable_normalized_inputs=sum(len(valid_residuals(e,a)) for e in(0,1) for a in range(128)))\\n\\ndef build_tables(cfg, search=2):\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"Optional credited exact-domain coefficient regeneration.\\n\\n    The radius policy is the published Kozykorpesh calibration, rather than\\n    the old continuous-domain fitter. The default selected build uses the\\n    explicitly published numeric table below, checked on every original x.\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    tables, report = regenerate_tables_exact(cfg)\\n    return tables, report['worst_ratio'] / 2**24\\n\\ndef layout(cfg, tabs, LB=21, S=20):\\n    d = np.arange(2**DB, dtype=np.int64)\\n    assert max(t[0] for t in tabs.values()) < (1 << cfg.w2)\\n    Rmax = 0; per = {}; Ymax = 0\\n    for key, (K2, K1, K0) in tabs.items():\\n        R, Q = pipeline(cfg, K2, K1, d)\\n        assert R.min() >= 0\\n        Rmax = max(Rmax, int(R.max()), K1)\\n        per[key] = (int(Q.min()), int(Q.max()))\\n        Ymax = max(Ymax, K0 - per[key][0])\\n    w1 = Rmax.bit_length()\\n    lo_tab, hi_tab = {}, {}\\n    for key, (K2, K1, K0) in tabs.items():\\n        qmin, qmax = per[key]\\n        base = K0 % (1 << S); j = 0\\n        while base + j * (1 << S) - qmax < 0: j += 1\\n        k0lo = base + j * (1 << S)\\n        assert k0lo - qmin < (1 << LB), 'layout infeasible'\\n        lo_tab[key] = k0lo; hi_tab[key] = (K0 - k0lo) >> S\\n        assert hi_tab[key] >= 0\\n    whi = max(max(hi_tab.values()).bit_length(), 1)\\n    return dict(w1=w1, LB=LB, S=S, whi=whi, lo=lo_tab, hi=hi_tab)\\n\\n# ----------------------------------------------------------------- circuit pieces\\ndef and_all_into(b, controls, target):\\n    k = len(controls)\\n    if k == 1: b.cx(controls[0], target); return\\n    if k == 2: b.ccx(controls[0], controls[1], target); return\\n    anc = b.alloc_qubits(k - 2)\\n    b.init_and(controls[0], controls[1], anc[0])\\n    for j in range(1, k - 2): b.init_and(anc[j - 1], controls[j + 1], anc[j])\\n    b.ccx(anc[k - 3], controls[k - 1], target)\\n    for j in reversed(range(1, k - 2)): b.del_and(anc[j - 1], controls[j + 1], anc[j])\\n    b.del_and(controls[0], controls[1], anc[0])\\n    b.free(anc)\\n\\ndef phase_all(b, controls):\\n    k = len(controls)\\n    if k == 1: b.z(controls[0]); return\\n    if k == 2: b.cz(controls[0], controls[1]); return\\n    anc = b.alloc_qubits(k - 2)\\n    b.init_and(controls[0], controls[1], anc[0])\\n    for j in range(1, k - 2): b.init_and(anc[j - 1], controls[j + 1], anc[j])\\n    b.cz(anc[k - 3], controls[k - 1])\\n    for j in reversed(range(1, k - 2)): b.del_and(anc[j - 1], controls[j + 1], anc[j])\\n    b.del_and(controls[0], controls[1], anc[0])\\n    b.free(anc)\\n\\nNSTAGES = [(4, 16), (3, 8), (2, 4), (1, 2), (0, 1)]\\n\\ndef _baseline_normalize(b, x, f):\\n    for (k, d) in NSTAGES:\\n        top = x[W - d:W]\\n        if d == 1:\\n            b.cx(x[W - 1], f[0]); b.x(f[0])\\n        else:\\n            b.x(top); and_all_into(b, list(top), f[k]); b.x(top)\\n        for i in reversed(range(0, W - d)): b.cswap(f[k], x[i], x[i + d])\\n\\n\\ndef _combined_normalize(b, x, f):\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"v3 step 1: f4, f3, f2 (the binary leading-zero count div 4) from one prefix-AND chain on the unshifted x.\\n    chain[m] = [x[W-m-2:W] == 0] (top m+2 bits zero); p_j = chain[4j-2] = [lz >= 4j].  f4 = p4, f3 = p2 ^ p4,\\n    f2 = p1 ^ p2 ^ p3 ^ p4 ^ p5 (nested prefixes, so XOR = bit 2 of lz).  19 ANDs instead of 15 + 7 + 3.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    bits = [x[W - 1 - m] for m in range(20)]                  # x21, x20, ..., x2\\n    b.x(km.array(bits))\\n    chain = b.alloc_qubits(19)\\n    b.init_and(bits[0], bits[1], chain[0])\\n    for m in range(1, 19): b.init_and(chain[m - 1], bits[m + 1], chain[m])\\n    p = {j: chain[4 * j - 2] for j in range(1, 6)}\\n    b.cx(p[4], f[4])\\n    b.cx(p[2], f[3]); b.cx(p[4], f[3])\\n    for j in range(1, 6): b.cx(p[j], f[2])\\n    for m in reversed(range(1, 19)): b.del_and(chain[m - 1], bits[m + 1], chain[m])\\n    b.del_and(bits[0], bits[1], chain[0])\\n    b.free(chain)\\n    b.x(km.array(bits))\\n    for (k, d) in NSTAGES:\\n        top = x[W - d:W]\\n        if d == 1:\\n            b.cx(x[W - 1], f[0]); b.x(f[0])\\n        elif d == 2:\\n            b.x(top); b.ccx(top[0], top[1], f[1]); b.x(top)\\n        for i in reversed(range(0, W - d)): b.cswap(f[k], x[i], x[i + d])\\n\\n# MAX_X < 3*2**20 proves the original two top bits are never both one.\\n# After inversion, their AND equals XOR with one, on every promised input.\\ndef normalize(b, x, f):\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"v3 step 1: f4, f3, f2 (the binary leading-zero count div 4) from one prefix-AND chain on the unshifted x.\\n    chain[m] = [x[W-m-2:W] == 0] (top m+2 bits zero); p_j = chain[4j-2] = [lz >= 4j].  f4 = p4, f3 = p2 ^ p4,\\n    f2 = p1 ^ p2 ^ p3 ^ p4 ^ p5 (nested prefixes, so XOR = bit 2 of lz).  19 ANDs instead of 15 + 7 + 3.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    bits = [x[W - 1 - m] for m in range(20)]                  # x21, x20, ..., x2\\n    b.x(km.array(bits))\\n    chain = b.alloc_qubits(19)\\n    b.x(chain[0]); b.cx(bits[0],chain[0]); b.cx(bits[1],chain[0])\\n    for m in range(1, 19): b.init_and(chain[m - 1], bits[m + 1], chain[m])\\n    p = {j: chain[4 * j - 2] for j in range(1, 6)}\\n    b.cx(p[4], f[4])\\n    b.cx(p[2], f[3]); b.cx(p[4], f[3])\\n    for j in range(1, 6): b.cx(p[j], f[2])\\n    for m in reversed(range(1, 19)): b.del_and(chain[m - 1], bits[m + 1], chain[m])\\n    b.cx(bits[1],chain[0]); b.cx(bits[0],chain[0]); b.x(chain[0])\\n    b.free(chain)\\n    b.x(km.array(bits))\\n    for (k, d) in NSTAGES:\\n        top = x[W - d:W]\\n        if d == 1:\\n            b.cx(x[W - 1], f[0]); b.x(f[0])\\n        elif d == 2:\\n            b.x(top); b.ccx(top[0], top[1], f[1]); b.x(top)\\n        for i in reversed(range(0, W - d)): b.cswap(f[k], x[i], x[i + d])\\n\\ndef _baseline_unnormalize(b, x, f):\\n    for (k, d) in reversed(NSTAGES):\\n        for i in range(0, W - d): b.cswap(f[k], x[i], x[i + d])\\n        top = x[W - d:W]\\n        if d == 1:\\n            b.x(f[0]); b.cx(x[W - 1], f[0])\\n        else:\\n            m = b.alloc_bits(1)[0]\\n            b.hmr(f[k], m)\\n            b.push_condition(m)                     # fixup runs on half the shots on average\\n            b.x(top); phase_all(b, list(top)); b.x(top)\\n            b.pop_condition()\\n            b.free(m)\\n\\ndef promise_phase_original_top(b,controls):\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"Phase the original top16-zero predicate using its promised bit relation.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    assert len(controls)==16\\n    controls=list(reversed(controls))\\n    k=len(controls)\\n    anc=b.alloc_qubits(k-2)\\n    b.x(anc[0]); b.cx(controls[0],anc[0]); b.cx(controls[1],anc[0])\\n    for j in range(1,k-2):\\n        b.init_and(anc[j-1],controls[j+1],anc[j])\\n    b.cz(anc[k-3],controls[k-1])\\n    for j in reversed(range(1,k-2)):\\n        b.del_and(anc[j-1],controls[j+1],anc[j])\\n    b.cx(controls[1],anc[0]); b.cx(controls[0],anc[0]); b.x(anc[0])\\n    b.free(anc)\\n\\ndef unnormalize(b, x, f):\\n    for (k, d) in reversed(NSTAGES):\\n        for i in range(0, W - d): b.cswap(f[k], x[i], x[i + d])\\n        top = x[W - d:W]\\n        if d == 1:\\n            b.x(f[0]); b.cx(x[W - 1], f[0])\\n        else:\\n            m = b.alloc_bits(1)[0]\\n            b.hmr(f[k], m)\\n            b.push_condition(m)                     # fixup runs on half the shots on average\\n            b.x(top)\\n            if d==16: promise_phase_original_top(b,list(top))\\n            else: phase_all(b,list(top))\\n            b.x(top)\\n            b.pop_condition()\\n            b.free(m)\\n\\ndef gadd(b, a, t, cin=None, sub=False):\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"t += a + cin (or t -= a + cin), Gidney adder: len(t)-1 Toffolis, uncompute by HMR.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    a = list(a); t = list(t); o = len(a); n = len(t)\\n    assert o <= n\\n    if sub: b.x(km.array(t))\\n    c = b.alloc_qubits(n - 1) if n > 1 else km.array([])\\n    def C(i): return cin if i == 0 else c[i - 1]\\n    for i in range(0, n - 1):\\n        if i == 0 and cin is None:\\n            if o: b.init_and(a[0], t[0], C(1))\\n        elif i < o:\\n            b.cx(C(i), a[i]); b.cx(C(i), t[i]); b.init_and(a[i], t[i], C(i + 1)); b.cx(C(i), C(i + 1))\\n        else:\\n            b.init_and(C(i), t[i], C(i + 1))\\n    if n - 1 < o: b.cx(a[n - 1], t[n - 1])\\n    if n > 1 or cin is not None: b.cx(C(n - 1), t[n - 1])\\n    for i in reversed(range(0, n - 1)):\\n        if i == 0 and cin is None:\\n            if o: b.del_and(a[0], t[0], C(1)); b.cx(a[0], t[0])\\n        elif i < o:\\n            b.cx(C(i), C(i + 1)); b.del_and(a[i], t[i], C(i + 1)); b.cx(C(i), a[i]); b.cx(a[i], t[i])\\n        else:\\n            b.del_and(C(i), t[i], C(i + 1)); b.cx(C(i), t[i])\\n    if n > 1: b.free(c)\\n    if sub: b.x(km.array(t))\\n\\ndef _baseline_shift_stages(b, f, reg, L, inverse):\\n    stages = [(1, 1), (2, 2), (3, 4), (4, 8)]\\n    occ = []; o = L\\n    for (k, d) in stages: occ.append(o); o = min(o + d, len(reg))\\n    seq = list(enumerate(stages))\\n    if not inverse:\\n        for idx, (k, d) in seq:\\n            for i in reversed(range(0, occ[idx])):\\n                if i + d < len(reg): b.cswap(f[k], reg[i], reg[i + d])\\n    else:\\n        for idx, (k, d) in reversed(seq):\\n            for i in range(0, occ[idx]):\\n                if i + d < len(reg): b.cswap(f[k], reg[i], reg[i + d])\\n\\n\\ndef shift_stages(b, f, reg, L, inverse):\\n    stages = [(1, 1), (2, 2), (3, 4), (4, 8)]\\n    occ = []; o = L\\n    for (k, d) in stages: occ.append(o); o = min(o + d, len(reg))\\n    seq = list(enumerate(stages))\\n    if not inverse:\\n        for idx, (k, d) in seq:\\n            for i in reversed(range(0, occ[idx])):\\n                if i + d >= len(reg): continue\\n                if i + d >= occ[idx]:                       # v2: target is |0> for every f\\n                    b.init_and(f[k], reg[i], reg[i + d]); b.cx(reg[i + d], reg[i])\\n                else:\\n                    b.cswap(f[k], reg[i], reg[i + d])\\n    else:\\n        for idx, (k, d) in reversed(seq):\\n            for i in range(0, occ[idx]):\\n                if i + d >= len(reg): continue\\n                if i + d >= occ[idx]:\\n                    b.cx(reg[i + d], reg[i]); b.del_and(f[k], reg[i], reg[i + d])\\n                else:\\n                    b.cswap(f[k], reg[i], reg[i + d])\\n\\ndef build_circuit(cfg, tabs, lay, adder=None):\\n    # The default preserves the public generator exactly. An injected adder\\n    # supports isolated, explicitly tested memory experiments.\\n    if adder is None: adder=gadd\\n    w2, w1, LB, S, whi = cfg.w2, lay['w1'], lay['LB'], lay['S'], lay['whi']\\n    gy, fy = cfg.gy, cfg.fy\\n    b = km.CircuitBuilder()\\n    x = b.create_quantum_register(W, name='x')\\n    f = b.alloc_qubits(5)\\n    c2 = b.alloc_qubits(w2); R = b.alloc_qubits(w1); Lr = b.alloc_qubits(LB); Yh = b.alloc_qubits(whi)\\n    tgt = km.array(list(Lr) + list(R) + list(c2) + list(Yh))\\n    table = [0] * 256\\n    for (e, a), (K2, K1, K0) in tabs.items():\\n        table[a + 128 * e] = lay['lo'][(e, a)] | (K1 << LB) | (K2 << (LB + w1)) | (lay['hi'][(e, a)] << (LB + w1 + w2))\\n    addr = km.array(list(x[14:21]) + [f[0]])\\n    delta = x[0:DB]\\n    def rrows(inverse):\\n        seq = cfg.rrow_list()\\n        if inverse: seq = list(reversed(seq))\\n        for i in seq:\\n            sh = cfg.sh2[i]\\n            if sh >= 0: off = c2[sh:w2]; t = R[0:w1]; cin = c2[sh - 1] if sh >= 1 else None\\n            else: off = c2[0:w2]; t = R[-sh:w1]; cin = None\\n            b.cx(delta[i], t); adder(b, off, t, cin, sub=inverse); b.cx(delta[i], t)\\n    def yrows(inverse):\\n        seq = list(reversed(range(DB)))\\n        if inverse: seq.reverse()\\n        for i in seq:\\n            sh = cfg.sh1[i]; assert 0 <= sh < w1\\n            t = Lr[0:LB]; cin = R[sh - 1] if sh >= 1 else None\\n            b.cx(delta[i], t); adder(b, R[sh:w1], t, cin, sub=inverse); b.cx(delta[i], t)\\n    normalize(b, x, f)\\n    b.init_lookup(table=table, address=addr, target=tgt)\\n    rrows(False); yrows(False)\\n    ext = b.alloc_qubits(10)\\n    if LB > S: adder(b, Lr[S:LB], Yh)\\n    Cx = km.array(list(Lr[gy:S]) + list(Yh) + list(ext))\\n    shift_stages(b, f, Cx, S - gy + whi, False)\\n    for j in range(len(Cx)):\\n        e = gy + j - fy - 10                       # bit weight 2^e turns\\n        if e < 0: b.z_pow(Cx[j], Fraction(2)**(e + 1))\\n    shift_stages(b, f, Cx, S - gy + whi, True)\\n    if LB > S: adder(b, Lr[S:LB], Yh, sub=True)\\n    b.free(ext)\\n    yrows(True); rrows(True)\\n    b.del_lookup(table=table, address=addr, target=tgt)\\n    b.free(tgt)\\n    unnormalize(b, x, f)\\n    b.free(f)\\n    return b.finish_circuit()\\n\\ndef main(out):\\n    cfg = Cfg(F2, F1, FYR, GY, DROP2)\\n    tabs, worst = build_tables(cfg)\\n    print(f'model worst relative error / 2^-24 = {worst * 2**24:.3f}')\\n    lay = layout(cfg, tabs)\\n    c = build_circuit(cfg, tabs, lay)\\n    s = c.operation_statistics()\\n    print('qubits', c.num_qubits, 'toffoli(static)', s.num_toffoli_like,\\n          'rotations', s.num_z_pow(min_clifford_hierarchy_level=4))\\n    c.to_file(out, format='kmb')\\n    print('wrote', out)\\n\\n\\ndef selected_cfg():\\n    return Cfg(F2,F1,FYR,GY,DROP2)\\n\\n\\ndef config_record(cfg):\\n    return dict(f2=cfg.f2,f1=cfg.f1,fyr=cfg.fyr,gy=cfg.gy,drop2=sorted(cfg.drop2))\\n\\n\\n_PUBLISHED_COEFFICIENT_ROWS = ((67, 184284, 189442910), (66, 182154, 188710050), (65, 180064, 187985630), (63, 178014, 187269499), (62, 176002, 186561483), (61, 174029, 185861435), (60, 172092, 185169210), (59, 170191, 184484662), (58, 168323, 183807650), (57, 166490, 183138038), (56, 164690, 182475692), (55, 162923, 181820481), (54, 161186, 181172277), (53, 159480, 180530959), (52, 157805, 179896401), (51, 156158, 179268491), (50, 154540, 178647108), (49, 152949, 178032144), (48, 151386, 177423486), (48, 149849, 176821020), (47, 148338, 176224658), (46, 146852, 175634293), (45, 145391, 175049819), (44, 143954, 174471143), (44, 142540, 173898157), (43, 141150, 173330791), (42, 139782, 172768943), (42, 138435, 172212512), (41, 137110, 171661436), (40, 135806, 171115614), (40, 134523, 170574956), (39, 133260, 170039402), (39, 132016, 169508850), (38, 130792, 168983243), (37, 129587, 168462497), (37, 128400, 167946525), (36, 127231, 167435275), (36, 126080, 166928657), (35, 124945, 166426619), (35, 123828, 165929073), (34, 122728, 165435976), (34, 121643, 164947236), (33, 120574, 164462814), (33, 119521, 163982624), (32, 118483, 163506626), (32, 117460, 163034740), (31, 116452, 162566926), (31, 115458, 162103106), (30, 114478, 161643244), (30, 113512, 161187263), (29, 112560, 160735129), (29, 111621, 160286771), (29, 110694, 159842141), (28, 109781, 159401203), (28, 108879, 158963885), (28, 107991, 158530145), (27, 107114, 158099945), (27, 106249, 157673220), (26, 105396, 157249942), (26, 104554, 156830046), (26, 103723, 156413494), (25, 102902, 156000254), (25, 102094, 155590262), (25, 101295, 155183486), (24, 100506, 154779894), (24, 99729, 154379426), (24, 98960, 153982048), (23, 98202, 153587734), (23, 97454, 153196425), (23, 96715, 152808089), (23, 95985, 152422695), (22, 95263, 152040209), (22, 94552, 151660581), (22, 93849, 151283781), (22, 93155, 150909775), (21, 92468, 150538541), (21, 91791, 150170021), (21, 91122, 149804197), (20, 90461, 149441041), (20, 89807, 149080